<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Maneuver glyph: ENTER ROUNDABOUT (GraphHopper Instruction.USE_ROUNDABOUT, sign 6).

     Construction, and why it is built this way - read before editing:

     1. THE RING IS NON-NEGOTIABLE. It is the only element that makes a glyph read as
        "roundabout" rather than "turn". Earlier attempts drew a hooked arrow with no ring and
        were indistinguishable from ic_maneuver_uturn_*.xml at 24dp.

     2. THE APPROACH ROAD IS TANGENT TO THE RING, NEVER RADIAL. The ring is centred at (15,13)
        with r=5, so its right tangent is exactly x=20 - which is where the approach road sits.
        This matters: a closed circle with a straight line through its centre reads as the Venus
        or Mars symbol, not as a road layout. Two earlier drafts failed exactly that way. Keep
        the road on the tangent.

     3. THE RING STAYS FULL WEIGHT IN BOTH ROUNDABOUT GLYPHS. An earlier draft de-emphasised the
        ring on this icon and on the LEAVE variant; at 24dp the faded ring disappeared and the
        glyph collapsed into a plain arrow, i.e. into "continue straight". ENTER and LEAVE are
        told apart by WHERE THE SOLID ARROWHEAD SITS, not by fading the ring:
          - ENTER (this file): arrowhead low, on the approach road, pointing up INTO the ring.
          - LEAVE (ic_maneuver_leave_roundabout.xml): arrowhead at the top, pointing up OUT of
            the ring; the approach road is the faded element instead.
        At 24dp the eye finds which END of the glyph carries the solid arrow well before it
        resolves any detail, so the pair separates even in peripheral vision.

     4. EXIT DIRECTION IS DRAWN STRAIGHT THROUGH, ON PURPOSE. This app cannot draw a specific
        exit: RouteInstruction carries only (sign, name, distanceMeters, durationMillis, points)
        - there is no exit-number field, so no per-exit variant could be selected even if one
        were drawn. If an exit number is added to that model later, this glyph is the base for a
        right / straight / left family.

     5. TRAFFIC HANDEDNESS. This app's primary market drives on the right, where roundabouts
        circulate counter-clockwise; entering on the right tangent and travelling up is exactly
        that direction. A left-hand-traffic build would need this pair mirrored horizontally.
        Record that decision rather than mirroring casually - mirroring a roundabout glyph
        changes the implied direction of travel, which is a different instruction.

     Colorless by design: the values below are neutral placeholders, NEVER the displayed color.
     Always render through ManeuverIcon.kt, which applies a theme-derived tint. A hardcoded
     Color.White / Color.Black at the call site is the exact bug this asset set exists to
     remove. -->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="32"
    android:viewportHeight="32">

    <!-- Onward road leaving the top of the ring, de-emphasised: it exists so the geometry reads
         as a through-route, but the instruction is about entering, not exiting. -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M15 8V4.6"
        android:strokeColor="#FF1B1B1B"
        android:strokeAlpha="0.42"
        android:strokeWidth="2.2"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:fillColor="#00000000" />

    <!-- The roundabout ring. Centre (15,13), r=5. Full weight in both roundabout glyphs. -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M15 8 A 5 5 0 0 1 15 18 A 5 5 0 0 1 15 8"
        android:strokeColor="#FF1B1B1B"
        android:strokeWidth="2.6"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:fillColor="#00000000" />

    <!-- Approach road, on the ring's right tangent (x=20). Full weight. -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M20 29V21.5"
        android:strokeColor="#FF1B1B1B"
        android:strokeWidth="2.6"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:fillColor="#00000000" />

    <!-- Arrowhead travelling up the approach road toward the ring: "you are about to enter". -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M20 15.5L16.6 21.5L23.4 21.5Z"
        android:fillColor="#FF1B1B1B" />
</vector>
